aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne -1$ or $s=-1.$\n\n- If $s\\ne -1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-1-s}{e}^{(-1-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>-1, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-(-1))t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le -1$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>-1$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1},$ defined for $s>-1.$" 13194139535376 true] [17592186346527 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346528 131941395353